-- title:
--   Lemma EC.7 — $P^h_m\le P^{NV(M)}(R^h_m)$ and $R^h_m\le R^{NV(M)}(P^h_m)$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds: $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12).
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 1–5 hold (Assumption 1 under $\mathbb P$ and $\mathbb P^M$; Assumptions 2–3 at a maximizer $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ of problem (2)) and $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$. Let $m\ge0$ and let $(P^h_m,R^h_m)$ be a minimizer of $B(m,\cdot,\cdot)$ over $D$ with $B(m,P^h_m,R^h_m)<\infty$. With the section maximizers
--   $$
--   P^{NV(M)}(R)=\arg\max_PV_0(P,R),\qquad R^{NV(M)}(P)=\arg\max_RV_0(P,R)\qquad (EC.8.2)
--   $$
--   taken over the sections $\{P:(P,R)\in D\}$ and $\{R:(P,R)\in D\}$ of the decision domain,
--   $$
--   P^h_m\le P^{NV(M)}(R^h_m),\qquad R^h_m\le R^{NV(M)}(P^h_m),
--   $$
--   for every such section maximizer.
--
--   It is the first step of the proofs of Propositions 5 and 7.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$; reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$: $\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\mathcal F_s]=\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\sigma(Y_s)]$ a.s. for $s\le t\le T$ and bounded measurable $g$; reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$ → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained only for $t\le T$; reason: Mathlib's Brownian motion lives on $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$; values after $T$ never enter
--   - encoding: stochastic integrals $\int\theta\,dX$, $\int b\,d\tilde B$, $\int\eta\,dB$ with almost-sure integrability → the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` (integrand cut off after $T$, square integrable on every path) plus a pathwise $du$-integral of the drift on every path; reason: under the augmented filtration an integrand can be set to zero on the exceptional null set without losing measurability, so the classes agree up to indistinguishability
--   - reading: for $u\le v$, $C_v-C_u$ is adapted to the σ-algebra generated by $\{X_t,u\le t\le v\}$ (p.11) → $C_v-C_u$ is measurable with respect to $\sigma\{X_t:u\le t\le v\}$, for $u\le v\le T$; reason: "adapted" for a single random variable means measurable
--   - encoding: $Z_t=e^{-\int_0^t\eta_s dB_s-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds}$ (12) → $Z_t=\exp(-I_t-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_u^2du)$ with $I$ a $\mathcal G$-adapted version of the Itô integral $\int\eta\,dB$; reason: an Itô integral is fixed only up to a null set; the paper uses that $Z$ is $\mathcal G$-adapted (p.ec23)
--   - reading: $(P^h_m,R^h_m):=\arg\min_{P,R}B(m,P,R)$ (15) → every minimizer $(P,R)$ of $B(m,\cdot,\cdot)$ over $D$; no minimizer is asserted to exist; reason: the stronger of the two readings; the proofs (pp.ec49–ec50) use only first-order and comparison arguments valid at any minimizer
--   - encoding: $(P^h_m,R^h_m)$ a minimizer of $B(m,\cdot,\cdot)$ (problem (14) presupposed feasible) → a minimizer with $B(m,P,R)<\infty$; reason: if $\mathbb P^M=\mathbb P$ and $m>\max V_0$, no hedge meets the constraint, the paper's $B$ is undefined and in $[0,\infty]$ every point would minimize $B\equiv\infty$; the finiteness excludes exactly that case
--   - encoding: the mean–variance problems (7), (13), (14) presuppose $\mathrm{Var}[H_T(P,R)]<\infty$ (p.9, p.ec23) → the hypothesis $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$ (`NegPartL2`); reason: with the Gaussian term $\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$ it is equivalent to $H_T(P,R)\in L^2(\mathbb P)$ for all $(P,R)$; it excludes only the case where problem (14) is undefined
--   - encoding: $B(m,P,R)$ the minimum of a variance over admissible hedges with mean $m$ (14) → an infimum in $[0,\infty]$ of the extended variance `evariance`; reason: an empty feasible set gives $+\infty$, not a junk $0$; attainment is part of Theorem 1
--   - reading: $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ the solution of problem (2), named by Assumptions 2–3 → any maximizer of $\mathbb E[H_T]$ over $D$ at which Assumptions 2 and 3 hold; reason: uniqueness is the content of Proposition 1 and is not built into a definition
--   - reading: $P^{NV(M)}(R):=\arg\max_PV_0(P,R)$, $R^{NV(M)}(P):=\arg\max_RV_0(P,R)$ (EC.8.2) → every maximizer over the section of $D$; reason: with the unconstrained formula $R^{NV(M)}(c)=F_M^{-1}(0)=-\infty$, and at a corner minimizer $(c,bc)$ the printed inequality would read $bc\le-\infty$; over the section $R^{NV(M)}(c)=bc$
--   - reading: "$(P^h_m,R^m_h)$" → $(P^h_m,R^h_m)$; reason: the paper's typo $R^m_h$
--   - encoding: "martingale" (relative to $\mathcal F_t$, footnote 4) → `IsMartingaleOn`: adapted, integrable, and the martingale identity for $s\le t\le T$; reason: the paper's martingales live on $[0,T]$; Mathlib's `Martingale` constrains all times
--   - encoding: "adapted" integrands $a,b,\gamma,\xi,\delta,\zeta$ → progressively measurable integrands; reason: the standard measurability of a stochastic integrand; on an augmented filtration an adapted process has a progressive modification
--   - encoding: an admissible strategy $\vartheta=\{\theta_t\}$ with $\chi_t(\vartheta)=\int_0^t\theta_s dX_s$ (EC.7.15) → a pair $(\theta,\chi)$ with $\chi$ an $\mathcal F$-adapted version of the gains on $[0,T]$; reason: the stochastic integral is fixed only up to null sets, so the strategy is carried with its gains; the terminal gains are the same random variable a.s.
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Lemma EC.7 and (EC.8.2), p.ec49 (PDF 85)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Hedging

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV

/-- Lemma EC.7, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.ec49: suppose Assumptions 1–5 (with `(P^NV, R^NV)` the
solution of problem (2)). For a target `m ≥ 0`, a minimizer `(P^h_m, R^h_m)` of `B(m, ·, ·)`
(15) satisfies `P^h_m ≤ P^NV(M)(R^h_m)` and `R^h_m ≤ R^NV(M)(P^h_m)`, where
`P^NV(M)(R) := arg max_P V_0(P,R)` and `R^NV(M)(P) := arg max_R V_0(P,R)` (EC.8.2).

Here `(P, R)` is any minimizer of `B(m, ·, ·)` over the decision domain with
`B(m,P,R) < ∞`, and the section maximizers are taken over the sections of the decision
domain: every maximizer `P'` of `V_0(·, R)` over `{P' : (P', R) ∈ D}` satisfies `P ≤ P'`, and
every maximizer `R'` of `V_0(P, ·)` over `{R' : (P, R') ∈ D}` satisfies `R ≤ R'`.

**Formalization Note** In the degenerate case `P^M = P` with `m` above the largest attainable
mean, problem (14) has no feasible hedge, the paper's `B` is undefined, and in `ℝ≥0∞` every
`(P, R)` would minimize `B ≡ ⊤`; the finiteness `B(m,P,R) < ∞` excludes exactly that case.
`NegPartL2` (`(C_T)^− ∈ L²(P)`) is the finite-variance presupposition of problem (14). -/
theorem lemma_ec_7 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hL2 : M.NegPartL2) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (h1P : Assumption1 (M.P.map M.AT)) (h1M : Assumption1 (M.PM.map M.AT))
    (PNV RNV : ℝ) (hNV : IsNVMaximizer p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (h2 : Assumption2 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) (h3 : Assumption3 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (m : ℝ) (hm : 0 ≤ m) (P R : ℝ) (hPR : (P, R) ∈ decisionDomain p)
    (hmin : ∀ P' R' : ℝ, (P', R') ∈ decisionDomain p → M.Bval m P R ≤ M.Bval m P' R')
    (hfin : M.Bval m P R < ⊤) :
    (∀ P' : ℝ, (P', R) ∈ decisionDomain p →
      (∀ P'' : ℝ, (P'', R) ∈ decisionDomain p → M.V0 P'' R ≤ M.V0 P' R) → P ≤ P') ∧
    (∀ R' : ℝ, (P, R') ∈ decisionDomain p →
      (∀ R'' : ℝ, (P, R'') ∈ decisionDomain p → M.V0 P R'' ≤ M.V0 P R') → R ≤ R') := by sorry

end HedgePSNV
